#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "7fc6630d-00e2-5528-a1d5-4c7f7b43e8a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-562" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) = 4t^2 - 3t^3,$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the velocity (in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s$) of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{4}{3}(0)^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = \\dfrac{4}{3}t^3 -\\dfrac{3}{4}t^4 + 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082930 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20159e8-8f86-5bf8-b87e-3eb990b819f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Not a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "9a655242-0547-5a0a-b4cc-2fb3caa11584" :question/math-academy-id "e-563" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) =2e^{-t}+6t^2$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-4 \\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the speed of the particle at the moment  $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{-t}+6{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(-{e}^{-t})+\\frac{6}{3}{t}^{3}+C \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-t}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0) = -4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-2{e}^{-0}+2\\cdot {0}^{3}+C \\\\ -4 & =-2+0+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = -2  e^{-t}+ 2t^3 -2.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =-2{e}^{-1}+2(1)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}+2-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{e}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\dfrac 2 e\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c38d0bd-2192-5e9b-b150-07a5086649a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289897} {:answer/id #uuid "74e83db5-8638-5454-8e90-b5e4296bc9a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289898} {:answer/id #uuid "56e7dee2-4197-57f1-8ebc-024d6d715bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289899} {:answer/id #uuid "93740cc5-d830-5826-b8d3-8383a5ef3da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289900} {:answer/id #uuid "b25f5106-8bb7-5827-85fb-54638328c9a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56e7dee2-4197-57f1-8ebc-024d6d715bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289899} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aabe25f-0df0-5c75-a151-4158c6a44722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbd286de-d18c-5c8c-8baf-fe50f2bdfc52" :question/math-academy-id "q-102693" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3+8t-3{t}^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3+8t-3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3t+4{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=-1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =3(0)+4(0)^{2}-(0)^{3}+C \\\\ -1 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3t+4{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}-1.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =3(2)+4(2)^{2}-(2)^{3}-1 \\\\  & =6+16-8-1 \\\\  & =13\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $13\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20159e8-8f86-5bf8-b87e-3eb990b819f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Not a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68635bc5-8612-5cca-8be1-80f8a4d3de21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289904} {:answer/id #uuid "8d189dfd-5fc4-5f1a-85b0-9474fffcd654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289905} {:answer/id #uuid "6e747da3-ae63-568a-8c91-04d46faaa91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289906} {:answer/id #uuid "d478c8eb-c35e-515e-b6b9-06132200075e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289907} {:answer/id #uuid "08cc742c-dd38-51b2-8359-afdcbed46083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d189dfd-5fc4-5f1a-85b0-9474fffcd654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289905} :answer-field/id #uuid "411ffd2b-061a-59ec-9cf3-f702d8c3d8a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ac29eea-5742-54dd-bfcd-cbd57bf8e08c" :question/math-academy-id "q-102742" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\sqrt{{t}^{3}},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {t}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{2}{5}(0)^{5/2}+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5/2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20159e8-8f86-5bf8-b87e-3eb990b819f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Not a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12366a0f-3ccf-575e-8a83-12fee14f805b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.33\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289911} {:answer/id #uuid "8622f8bd-e5c3-5c92-9ede-f40b3f102e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289912} {:answer/id #uuid "f0656c44-8d51-5fb2-bb3c-6a76fa1f8b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289913} {:answer/id #uuid "88f59713-2416-5173-be6f-cefdba3b6416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289914} {:answer/id #uuid "b92273d9-ab8e-5ecb-9f15-bb56758d7924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0656c44-8d51-5fb2-bb3c-6a76fa1f8b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289913} :answer-field/id #uuid "e52358ec-5418-582d-84ec-876052a9389f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e694ce3-05d1-58d3-bfbc-b58138322825" :question/math-academy-id "q-19487" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.75\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa0f6412-9d9a-5e5f-b235-30bf39102742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^7+3t^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289918} {:answer/id #uuid "438fe4b3-62f1-5533-a20c-d8669ccf09e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^7+3t^4+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "438fe4b3-62f1-5533-a20c-d8669ccf09e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^7+3t^4+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289919} :answer-field/id #uuid "a309ad20-6987-57d0-b45b-abe948c42042" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e1638b5-7055-56e8-8524-6658a022f221" :question/math-academy-id "q-350978" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=14{t}^{6}+12{t}^{3},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=310\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (14{t}^{6}+12{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{7}+3{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=310.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}310 & =2({2}^{7})+3({2}^{4})+C \\\\ 310 & =304+C \\\\ C & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{7}+3{t}^{4}+6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caa4a2ee-dbd2-545c-b653-1e69d669e019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caa4a2ee-dbd2-545c-b653-1e69d669e019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289922} :answer-field/id #uuid "65e9b977-6d14-5d8a-b77c-1273009241ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e948bb7d-f7d3-5c00-b9a6-b51b039f48d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-351011" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{ft/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=8{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=-4\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (8{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=-4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2({0}^{4})-2({0}^{3})+C \\\\ -4 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}-4.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=3\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =2({3}^{4})-2({3}^{3})-4 \\\\  & =2(81)-2(27)-4 \\\\  & =162-54-4 \\\\  & =104\\,\\text{ft/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $104\\,\\text{ft/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20159e8-8f86-5bf8-b87e-3eb990b819f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Not a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d5488f4-d374-58ea-8bc4-f38768158635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d5488f4-d374-58ea-8bc4-f38768158635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289925} :answer-field/id #uuid "53be91c7-9f20-5aab-8680-c267e0647063" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2da25941-d469-57cc-a3a6-d483c3ddfc08" :question/math-academy-id "q-351060" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=-\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int -\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{4}{0+1}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =4+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{t+1}-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{4}{1+1}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e65870-2b27-5894-a0a0-203908584a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289928} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd80d09-4e56-593c-93b9-b51f7f9c1c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289929} {:answer/id #uuid "71da9d86-9793-50ad-bd48-53f7e54f5912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289930} {:answer/id #uuid "64ea1d6b-87e4-549e-8533-49aa0fdf4c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289931} {:answer/id #uuid "8e305e54-abfc-5bff-96e1-5a8ab1c1d05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71da9d86-9793-50ad-bd48-53f7e54f5912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289930} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0556b96-5033-5370-8905-0acdcd83681b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f8fadaa-0d78-521b-ae7c-e7c1f771ff78" :question/math-academy-id "q-81474" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=6{t}^{2}-2t-10$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6{t}^{2}-2t-10)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-10t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=-4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}-10(1)+C \\\\ -4 & =2-1-10+C \\\\ C & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-10t+5.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=3\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =2(3)^{3}-(3)^{2}-10(3)+5 \\\\  & =54-9-30+5 \\\\  & =20\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $20\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ac861a-ab7c-53ce-8bd5-5601d38d5f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t)=2{t}^{3}-{t}^{4}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289935} {:answer/id #uuid "193973e4-f5ec-5fe8-97f7-a954ec7508b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t)=3{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289936} {:answer/id #uuid "9df47581-cae3-5199-9399-446ebf23d5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t)=3{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289937} {:answer/id #uuid "dc333160-e9c2-5c93-b59f-ce794a23b04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t)=6{t}^{3}-4{t}^{4}+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289938} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d58bf4-572b-5eeb-9eb2-be7662cc1916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t)=2{t}^{3}-{t}^{4}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3d58bf4-572b-5eeb-9eb2-be7662cc1916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t)=2{t}^{3}-{t}^{4}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289939} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7d7e64f-ed22-5c5c-8cc9-ad5ae6fbc63a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "046b01cc-67cf-5c60-952b-af082bd5d5da" :question/math-academy-id "q-81684" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{ft}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=6{t}^{2}-4{t}^{3},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity (in $\\text{ft/s}$) of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6{t}^{2}-4{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}-{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=-5.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =2(2)^{3}-(2)^{4}+C \\\\ -5 & =16-16+C \\\\ C & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-{t}^{4}-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20159e8-8f86-5bf8-b87e-3eb990b819f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Not a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83777860-0064-5ae2-b5e5-0be5d41c2b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t)=4{e}^{-2t}-27{e}^{-4t}+24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289942} {:answer/id #uuid "ec81c54e-b22b-59b5-98e9-0508a7728308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t)=4{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289943} {:answer/id #uuid "85c30624-87ef-5db2-acd3-2cea9071754d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t)=4{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289944} {:answer/id #uuid "48e012c2-aa4f-5fcc-88b1-453915e7d7fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t)=4{e}^{-t}-27{e}^{-3t}+24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289945} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa1e3ed-9b96-5706-8a7a-7d6ae8f03a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t)=4-3{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec81c54e-b22b-59b5-98e9-0508a7728308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t)=4{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289943} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfd6d054-fece-5ffd-a711-5d40a53dfbc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "adaf0a1a-e89f-5330-af89-83ce6c6e01c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81709" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=9{e}^{-3t}-4{e}^{-t}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity (in $\\text{m/s}$)  of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (9{e}^{-3t}-4{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})-4(-{e}^{-t})+C \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3t}+4{e}^{-t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-3{e}^{0}+4{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 1 & =-3+4+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=4{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20159e8-8f86-5bf8-b87e-3eb990b819f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Not a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cecfbe4f-d6cd-574b-a887-60c20f75af65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289949} {:answer/id #uuid "1614ac07-63ff-5d36-8b64-acacbcb5c27b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289950} {:answer/id #uuid "eda28035-baf7-523a-9638-4173ed32875f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289951} {:answer/id #uuid "43ddfe83-f8d4-52b4-8ce5-6ca02300c4a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289952} {:answer/id #uuid "540c8d32-1691-5eb9-9022-655ecc1644b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1614ac07-63ff-5d36-8b64-acacbcb5c27b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289950} :answer-field/id #uuid "62cdb047-288d-5b95-82e5-eb922356d91f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "733f11d7-9615-5c7c-9d3b-78f5e942f1ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-81710" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{ft}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+C \\\\ 3 & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 3 & =-2\\cdot 0+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =3-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$"}]}
